# Gitrules — git conventions
## Why these four together
Branch, commit, PR, and secrets **are called at the same moments.** You branch
when starting, commit and open a PR when finishing, and check for leaked secrets
in between. Kept apart they cost four always-on descriptions and still fail to
be read together.
## Branches — `<type>/<kebab-case>`
```
feat/add-model-version-checker # new feature
fix/emoji-detection-bug # bug fix
refactor/update-helper-logic # restructuring
docs/add-development-guide # documentation
test/hook-coverage # tests
chore/update-omc-hot-paths # build, config
```
| Type | When |
|---|---|
| `feat` | New feature |
| `fix` | Bug fix |
| `refactor` | Structure changes with no behaviour change |
| `docs` | Documentation |
| `test` | Tests |
| `chore` | Build, config, dependencies |
**No usernames** (`jaesol-shin/...` is out). Type and name are separated by a
**slash** (not `feature-new-plugin`).
**The default branch is exempt.** `master` carries no type prefix because the
rule is about *work* branches — there is nothing to name it after. The checker
does not know this and reports `master` as malformed; the gap is in the checker,
not in the rule.
Work branches use the two-part `<type>/<desc>` form that `CLAUDE.md` names as
this repository's convention (`docs/add-development-guide`,
`chore/update-omc-hot-paths`). Upstream `session-and-git` documents a
`<folder>/<category>/<desc>` form (`web/feature/...`); it is kept for reference
at `references/branch.md`. Do not create new branches in it.
## Commits — Conventional Commits
```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>
<body>
<footer>
```
`(scope)` is optional — for plugin work use `plugins`, `git`, `model-checker`.
**Subject** — 50 characters or fewer, no trailing period, ends with an action
verb (add, modify, change, or remove). This is the one place the subject
length is set; other skills and commands point here instead of restating it.
**Body** — separated from the subject by a blank line; the first paragraph is
**why** (problem, motivation), the second is **how** (approach). Wrap at 72.
**Never advertise the tool, and never add an AI co-author trailer.**
`Generated with Claude Code` and `Co-Authored-By: Claude ...` are both out,
including a trailer a harness adds on its own; remove it before committing.
`scripts/check_commit.js` rejects both phrases, the bracketed
`Generated with [Claude Code]` form, and `Claude-Session:` trailers, whatever
their case, when a line starts with one (a footer may follow an emoji); a
sentence that quotes a phrase mid-line passes. A `Co-Authored-By` line is an AI
only with an `anthropic.com` address, or the name `Claude` alone or followed by
model words and version numbers; a human co-author such as `Claude Monet` is not
rejected. The `block_ai_attribution` hook denies a `git commit` or
`gh pr create` that carries any of them, in every repository where jstack is
enabled, because skill text alone did not stop Claude Code's default trailer.
Claude Code's own `attribution` setting stops them at the source.
Types match the branch list plus `style`, `remove`, and `perf`. The upstream
original — full table plus body examples — is kept at `references/commit.md`.
## PRs — the repository owns the label rule
```bash
gh pr create --title "feat(plugins): ..." --label "feature" --label "@web" --body "..."
```
**The rule is not written here.** It lives in `.claude/pr-policy.json`, per
repository, and `validate_pr_label.py` reads it at `gh pr create` time. A rule
written into this skill would be this organization's rule applied to every
repository the plugin is installed in — and it would silently do nothing
outside one GitHub owner.
The policy file records both the rule and the evidence for it: which documents
declared it, how many PRs were observed, and at what rate. That is what a later
reader needs to answer "why is this label required".
| The file says | Then |
| --- | --- |
| Required label groups | Missing one blocks the PR |
| `rules: []` | Nothing is enforced; this repository does not label |
| `enforced: false` | The evidence was weak — it warns and lets the PR through |
**The file must exist before a PR can be opened**, even empty. Empty means
someone decided this repository has no label policy; absent means nobody has
looked. The two are not the same, and only one of them should let a PR out.
For this repository the discovered policy is two groups — one `@`-prefixed
position and one plain category. Read the current values from the file or from
`gh label list`, not from this paragraph; a list copied into prose goes stale
without anyone noticing.
The upstream original — the full label tables it used to declare as the rule —
is kept at `references/pr.md`, demoted to an example of this repository's
observed values. `@package` is dropped from it: `.claude/pr-policy.json`
records it as a `phantom_labels` entry, not as an allowed one.
The body carries two sections: `## Summary` and `## Test plan`.
## Secrets — stop them before the commit
`.gitignore` is a **repository-wide** rule; `.git/info/exclude` is **local only**.
Personal settings nobody else needs go in the latter.
**A secret already committed is not fixed by rewriting history** — revoke and
reissue it immediately. Tool choices are in `references/secrets.md`.
## Checking
Requires Node.js 20+.
```bash
node ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/gitrules/scripts/check_commit.js --staged
node ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/gitrules/scripts/check_commit.js --branch
node ${JSTACK_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/gitrules/scripts/check_commit.js --message "feat: 무엇을 추가"
```
**The checker sees format** — type, length, banned phrases, branch shape.
Whether the body actually explains *why* is read by a person.
### Organization overrides
An organization profile can carry `conventions.json` under
`~/.jstack/profiles/<name>/`: commit language, allowed commit and branch types,
subject and body limits, a Korean action-noun ending, the `Resolves: #<n>`
footer, and the automatic commit language. The checker applies the profile named
by `JSTACK_PROFILE`, else the default profile, and names the source in its verdict;
without one it applies the rules above. `check_commit.js --conventions` prints the active
values, and `--defaults` ignores the profile for one check.
When the profile's commit or automatic commit language disagrees with this
repository's recent history, a message check and `--conventions` exit 3 and print
the current state, a recommendation and how to roll back; branch checks still run.
**Ask the user and record the answer** with `check_commit.js --choose repository`
or `--choose profile`; never choose for them. The choice holds for this repository
and its linked worktrees until the effective conventions change (a profile edit
or a jstack update of the defaults) or the history turns to the other language. A legacy
`<folder>/<category>/<desc>` branch scheme in a profile is recorded, not enforced.
## Boundaries
| Task | Owner |
|---|---|
| Session wrap-up, retrospective, next steps | `/wrap` command |
| Running the commit itself | `/commit` command |
| Opening the PR | `/pr` command |
| A credential that is already in the repository | `secrets` skill |
| Registering the PR policy in a new repository | `setup` skill |
| `gh` missing or signed out | `bridge` skill |
## Limits (stated)
- The checker sees **format**. It cannot measure whether the body explains why and how
- Secret *detection* is not in this skill — use `gitleaks`; the choice is in the reference
- PR label rules come from `.claude/pr-policy.json`, discovered per repository — this skill does not carry the values
- Policy discovery needs `gh`; without it a registered policy still enforces but changes to it go undetected (the `bridge` skill's probe recipes — that reference lives there, not here)
- The default-branch exemption is stated wider than the checker implements: `--branch` still calls `master` malformed. The gap is in the checker; the rule is what this document says
